Why this and not an open licence. The second tree published everything, and it was right to:
a format for parties who do not trust each other, with a closed implementation, is not a format.
This tree is not that. It is a served product, and there is nothing it gains by being copyable
that it does not lose by being operated by somebody who promises nothing.

What is meant to be public is the pair of specifications: the record a dataset produces and the
interface it answers. They are what a dataset somebody else operates would have to satisfy, and
they will be published as documentation on the day the first one does. Until then nobody needs
them and publishing them would be an announcement rather than a document.

"Zetlyn" is a name, not a licence to use it.
